/* generated by tools/lite.js - do not edit. The questions (and sets) that the verbal lessons quiz blocks use. */
QB.add([
  {"id":"reading-comprehension-01","sec":"verbal","topic":"reading-comprehension","pattern":"main-idea-title","d":"Medium","q":"Which of the following best expresses the central idea of the passage?","opts":["Spreadsheets destroyed many accounting careers in the 1980s.","Whether automation helps or hurts work depends on how organisations redesign roles and training around it.","Companies should avoid new software to protect their staff.","Senior experts are more valuable than junior employees."],"ans":1,"time":60,"sol":["The passage argues that effects depend on how work is organised, including entry-level training."],"short":"The passage argues that effects depend on how work is organised, including entry-level training.","trap":"Picking a detail (spreadsheets) as the main idea.","why":["Reverses what the passage says about spreadsheets.",null,"The author says firms should not resist the technology.","A side point, not the theme."],"set":"rc-1"},
  {"id":"reading-comprehension-02","sec":"verbal","topic":"reading-comprehension","pattern":"specific-detail","d":"Easy","q":"According to the passage, why did the number of financial analysts grow after spreadsheets arrived?","opts":["Accountants were forced to change their jobs.","Firms stopped calculating by hand.","Cheaper calculations led firms to ask for more of them.","Spreadsheets required more people to operate them."],"ans":2,"time":45,"sol":["Stated in paragraph 1: calculations became cheaper, so firms wanted more."],"short":"Stated in paragraph 1: calculations became cheaper, so firms wanted more.","trap":"Choosing a plausible reason that is not stated.","why":["Not stated.","Not stated.",null,"Not stated; spreadsheets sped tasks up."],"set":"rc-1"},
  {"id":"reading-comprehension-03","sec":"verbal","topic":"reading-comprehension","pattern":"inference","d":"Hard","q":"Which of the following can be inferred from the second paragraph?","opts":["Repetitive supervised tasks have worked as a training ground for juniors, so losing them could leave a skills gap.","Senior experts will soon be replaced by software.","Junior employees are less capable than automated tools.","Training new employees is no longer necessary."],"ans":0,"time":90,"sol":["The paragraph says juniors learned by doing repetitive work, so removing it threatens training."],"short":"The paragraph says juniors learned by doing repetitive work, so removing it threatens training.","trap":"Over-reading the passage into claims about replacing seniors.","why":[null,"Not implied; seniors are described as plentiful.","Not implied.","Contradicts the concern raised."],"set":"rc-1"},
  {"id":"reading-comprehension-04","sec":"verbal","topic":"reading-comprehension","pattern":"vocabulary-in-context","d":"Easy","q":"In the second paragraph, \"frame\" (a task a human \"had to frame\") most nearly means:","opts":["place within a border","falsely accuse","photograph","formulate"],"ans":3,"time":45,"sol":["\"Frame a task\" means to set it up or formulate it."],"short":"\"Frame a task\" means to set it up or formulate it.","trap":"Choosing the literal meaning.","why":["Literal meaning.","Different sense of the word.","Different sense of the word.",null],"set":"rc-1"},
  {"id":"reading-comprehension-05","sec":"verbal","topic":"reading-comprehension","pattern":"true-false-cannot-say","d":"Hard","q":"Statement: \"Most firms using automation software have reduced their workforce.\" Based on the passage, this statement is:","opts":["True","False","Cannot say","Partly true"],"ans":2,"time":90,"sol":["The passage says nothing about overall workforce reductions."],"short":"The passage says nothing about overall workforce reductions.","trap":"Treating a plausible claim as supported.","why":["No such claim is made.","No such claim is contradicted.",null,"Nothing is said either way."],"set":"rc-1"},
  {"id":"error-spotting-01","sec":"verbal","topic":"error-spotting","pattern":"subject-verb-agreement","d":"Easy","q":"Find the part of the sentence that has an error. If there is no error, choose \"No error\".\n\n(A) A number of passengers is\n(B) waiting at the platform\n(C) for the delayed train.","opts":["A","B","C","No error"],"ans":0,"time":35,"sol":["\"A number of\" takes a plural verb: \"are waiting\"."],"short":"\"A number of\" takes a plural verb: \"are waiting\".","trap":"\"The number of\" is singular, but \"a number of\" means \"several\".","why":[null,"Fine: a plain participle phrase.","Fine: correct preposition and adjective.","There is an error in (A)."]},
  {"id":"error-spotting-02","sec":"verbal","topic":"error-spotting","pattern":"tenses","d":"Easy","q":"Find the part of the sentence that has an error. If there is no error, choose \"No error\".\n\n(A) I am knowing him\n(B) since we studied\n(C) in the same college.","opts":["A","B","C","No error"],"ans":0,"time":35,"sol":["Stative verbs like \"know\" are not used in continuous tense; use \"have known\"."],"short":"Stative verbs like \"know\" are not used in continuous tense; use \"have known\".","trap":"Using the present continuous with \"know\" because the sentence talks about now.","why":[null,"Fine: simple past after \"since\".","Fine.","There is an error in (A)."]},
  {"id":"error-spotting-04","sec":"verbal","topic":"error-spotting","pattern":"prepositions","d":"Medium","q":"Find the part of the sentence that has an error. If there is no error, choose \"No error\".\n\n(A) Our team leader is senior than me\n(B) in age, but she joined\n(C) the company later than I did.","opts":["A","B","C","No error"],"ans":0,"time":50,"sol":["\"Senior\", \"junior\", \"superior\", \"inferior\" take \"to\", not \"than\": \"senior to me\"."],"short":"\"Senior\", \"junior\", \"superior\", \"inferior\" take \"to\", not \"than\": \"senior to me\".","trap":"Treating \"senior\" like an ordinary comparative.","why":[null,"Fine.","Fine: \"later than\" is a real comparative.","There is an error in (A)."]},
  {"id":"error-spotting-06","sec":"verbal","topic":"error-spotting","pattern":"parallelism","d":"Medium","q":"Find the part of the sentence that has an error. If there is no error, choose \"No error\".\n\n(A) The new policy aims at\n(B) cutting costs, improving quality,\n(C) and to increase customer satisfaction.","opts":["A","B","C","No error"],"ans":2,"time":50,"sol":["Items in a list must share one form: \"cutting ... improving ... and increasing\"."],"short":"Items in a list must share one form: \"cutting ... improving ... and increasing\".","trap":"Switching from -ing forms to a to-infinitive in the last item.","why":["Fine.","Fine: two parallel -ing forms.",null,"There is an error in (C)."]},
  {"id":"error-spotting-09","sec":"verbal","topic":"error-spotting","pattern":"degrees-of-comparison","d":"Hard","q":"Find the part of the sentence that has an error. If there is no error, choose \"No error\".\n\n(A) Of the two proposals submitted,\n(B) the first is cheaper,\n(C) but the second is the most practical.","opts":["A","B","C","No error"],"ans":2,"time":75,"sol":["For two items use the comparative: \"the more practical\"."],"short":"For two items use the comparative: \"the more practical\".","trap":"Using the superlative when only two things are compared.","why":["Fine.","Fine: comparative for two.",null,"There is an error in (C)."]},
  {"id":"sentence-correction-01","sec":"verbal","topic":"sentence-correction","pattern":"replace-the-underlined-part","d":"Easy","q":"Choose the option that best replaces the **underlined** part (shown in bold). If the sentence is already correct, choose \"No improvement\".\n\nNeither of the two candidates **were** selected for the final round.","opts":["were not","was","are","have been"],"ans":1,"time":35,"sol":["\"Neither\" is singular, so it takes \"was\"."],"short":"\"Neither\" is singular, so it takes \"was\".","trap":"Matching the verb to \"candidates\" instead of \"Neither\".","why":["Double negative: \"neither\" already negates.",null,"Wrong tense for a completed selection.","Wrong tense and still plural."]},
  {"id":"sentence-correction-03","sec":"verbal","topic":"sentence-correction","pattern":"no-improvement","d":"Easy","q":"Choose the option that best replaces the **underlined** part (shown in bold). If the sentence is already correct, choose \"No improvement\".\n\nThe teacher, along with her students, **is preparing** for the annual day.","opts":["No improvement","are preparing","were preparing","have prepared"],"ans":0,"time":35,"sol":["\"Along with\" does not change the number: the verb agrees with \"teacher\", so \"is\"."],"short":"\"Along with\" does not change the number: the verb agrees with \"teacher\", so \"is\".","trap":"Letting the nearer noun \"students\" decide the verb.","why":[null,"Agrees with \"students\", not the subject \"teacher\".","Wrong tense and number.","Changes the tense and number."]},
  {"id":"sentence-correction-05","sec":"verbal","topic":"sentence-correction","pattern":"replace-the-underlined-part","d":"Medium","q":"Choose the option that best replaces the **underlined** part (shown in bold). If the sentence is already correct, choose \"No improvement\".\n\nHaving reached the station, **the train had already left**.","opts":["we found that the train had already left","the train was already left","No improvement","the train already left by then"],"ans":0,"time":50,"sol":["A participle phrase must modify the subject of the main clause: \"we\", not \"the train\"."],"short":"A participle phrase must modify the subject of the main clause: \"we\", not \"the train\".","trap":"Dangling modifier: the train did not reach the station.","why":[null,"Passive form is wrong, and the modifier still dangles.","The phrase would say the train reached the station.","Same dangling problem."]},
  {"id":"sentence-correction-06","sec":"verbal","topic":"sentence-correction","pattern":"replace-the-underlined-part","d":"Medium","q":"Choose the option that best replaces the **underlined** part (shown in bold). If the sentence is already correct, choose \"No improvement\".\n\nIf I **was** you, I would accept the offer.","opts":["am","were","had been","No improvement"],"ans":1,"time":50,"sol":["Imaginary present conditions use the subjunctive \"were\" for all persons."],"short":"Imaginary present conditions use the subjunctive \"were\" for all persons.","trap":"Using \"was\" in an unreal condition.","why":["Not possible: an unreal condition needs the subjunctive.",null,"Past perfect suits a past unreal condition, not this one.","\"was\" is not the subjunctive."]},
  {"id":"sentence-correction-09","sec":"verbal","topic":"sentence-correction","pattern":"no-improvement","d":"Hard","q":"Choose the option that best replaces the **underlined** part (shown in bold). If the sentence is already correct, choose \"No improvement\".\n\nUnless you **work hard, you will not clear** the aptitude test.","opts":["do not work hard, you will not clear","No improvement","will work hard, you will not clear","worked hard, you will not clear"],"ans":1,"time":75,"sol":["\"Unless\" already means \"if not\", so the clause after it stays affirmative."],"short":"\"Unless\" already means \"if not\", so the clause after it stays affirmative.","trap":"Adding a second negative after \"unless\".","why":["Double negative with \"unless\".",null,"\"will\" is not used in an \"unless\" clause.","Past tense does not fit a future condition."]},
  {"id":"voice-01","sec":"verbal","topic":"voice","pattern":"active-passive-simple-tenses","d":"Easy","q":"Choose the correct passive form of the sentence.\n\nThe chef prepared the meal.","opts":["The meal is prepared by the chef.","The meal has been prepared by the chef.","The meal was prepared by the chef.","The meal were prepared by the chef."],"ans":2,"time":35,"sol":["Simple past active becomes \"was/were + past participle\"."],"short":"Simple past active becomes \"was/were + past participle\".","trap":"Changing the tense during conversion.","why":["Present tense instead of past.","Present perfect instead of past.",null,"\"meal\" is singular: \"was\", not \"were\"."]},
  {"id":"voice-03","sec":"verbal","topic":"voice","pattern":"active-passive-questions","d":"Easy","q":"Choose the correct passive form of the sentence.\n\nDid the manager sign the letter?","opts":["Is the letter signed by the manager?","Was the letter signed by the manager?","Did the letter sign by the manager?","Was the letter signing by the manager?"],"ans":1,"time":35,"sol":["\"Did + V1\" questions become \"Was/Were + object + past participle?\"."],"short":"\"Did + V1\" questions become \"Was/Were + object + past participle?\".","trap":"Keeping \"did\" in the passive.","why":["Present tense.",null,"\"did\" does not belong in the passive.","Wrong form of the verb."]},
  {"id":"voice-04","sec":"verbal","topic":"voice","pattern":"active-passive-perfect-and-continuous","d":"Medium","q":"Choose the correct passive form of the sentence.\n\nThe committee has approved the new proposal.","opts":["The new proposal was approved by the committee.","The new proposal is approved by the committee.","The new proposal had been approved by the committee.","The new proposal has been approved by the committee."],"ans":3,"time":50,"sol":["Present perfect passive: \"has/have been + past participle\"."],"short":"Present perfect passive: \"has/have been + past participle\".","trap":"Dropping the perfect aspect.","why":["Simple past changes the tense.","Simple present changes the tense.","Past perfect changes the tense.",null]},
  {"id":"voice-07","sec":"verbal","topic":"voice","pattern":"active-passive-modals","d":"Medium","q":"Choose the correct passive form of the sentence.\n\nYou must submit the form by Friday.","opts":["The form must submitted by you by Friday.","The form must be submitted by you by Friday.","The form must been submitted by you by Friday.","The form should be submitted by you by Friday."],"ans":1,"time":50,"sol":["Modal passive: \"modal + be + past participle\"."],"short":"Modal passive: \"modal + be + past participle\".","trap":"Leaving out \"be\".","why":["\"be\" is missing.",null,"\"been\" is wrong after \"must\".","\"should\" changes the modal."]},
  {"id":"voice-09","sec":"verbal","topic":"voice","pattern":"active-passive-imperatives","d":"Hard","q":"Choose the correct passive form of the sentence.\n\nSwitch off the lights before you leave the room.","opts":["Let the lights be switched off before you leave the room.","The lights are switched off before you leave the room.","Let the lights switched off before you leave the room.","The lights should switch off before you leave the room."],"ans":0,"time":75,"sol":["An imperative becomes \"Let + object + be + past participle\"."],"short":"An imperative becomes \"Let + object + be + past participle\".","trap":"Turning a command into a plain statement.","why":[null,"A statement, not a command.","\"be\" is missing after \"Let\".","\"switch off\" is active in form."]},
  {"id":"narration-01","sec":"verbal","topic":"narration","pattern":"direct-indirect-statements","d":"Easy","q":"Choose the correct reported speech for the sentence.\n\nHe said, \"I am tired.\"","opts":["He said that he has been tired.","He said that I was tired.","He said that he was tired.","He said me that he was tired."],"ans":2,"time":35,"sol":["Present simple backshifts to past simple; \"I\" becomes \"he\"."],"short":"Present simple backshifts to past simple; \"I\" becomes \"he\".","trap":"Leaving the pronoun or tense unchanged.","why":["Wrong tense.","\"I\" should become \"he\".",null,"\"said me\" is wrong; \"said to me\" or \"told me\"."]},
  {"id":"narration-04","sec":"verbal","topic":"narration","pattern":"direct-indirect-questions","d":"Easy","q":"Choose the correct reported speech for the sentence.\n\nHe said to me, \"Where do you live?\"","opts":["He asked me where do I live.","He asked me where I lived.","He asked me where you lived.","He asked me that where I lived."],"ans":1,"time":35,"sol":["Questions become statements in reported speech: no inversion, no \"that\"."],"short":"Questions become statements in reported speech: no inversion, no \"that\".","trap":"Keeping the question order.","why":["Question order kept.",null,"\"you\" should become \"I\".","\"that\" is not used with wh-questions."]},
  {"id":"narration-07","sec":"verbal","topic":"narration","pattern":"direct-indirect-commands","d":"Medium","q":"Choose the correct reported speech for the sentence.\n\nThe officer said to the clerk, \"Send the file immediately.\"","opts":["The officer said to the clerk that send the file immediately.","The officer ordered the clerk he sent the file immediately.","The officer ordered the clerk to send the file immediately.","The officer said the clerk to send the file immediately."],"ans":2,"time":50,"sol":["Commands use a reporting verb like ordered/told plus a to-infinitive."],"short":"Commands use a reporting verb like ordered/told plus a to-infinitive.","trap":"Using \"that\" with a command.","why":["Command kept inside a that-clause.","Missing the to-infinitive.",null,"\"said\" is the wrong verb here."]},
  {"id":"narration-09","sec":"verbal","topic":"narration","pattern":"direct-indirect-exclamations","d":"Medium","q":"Choose the correct reported speech for the sentence.\n\nHe said, \"Alas! I have lost my wallet.\"","opts":["He exclaimed that alas he has lost his wallet.","He exclaimed sorrowfully that he had lost his wallet.","He said alas that I had lost my wallet.","He exclaimed with sorrow that I had lost his wallet."],"ans":1,"time":50,"sol":["\"Alas!\" becomes \"exclaimed with sorrow\" (or sorrowfully) and the interjection is dropped."],"short":"\"Alas!\" becomes \"exclaimed with sorrow\" (or sorrowfully) and the interjection is dropped.","trap":"Keeping the interjection.","why":["Interjection kept and tense not backshifted.",null,"Interjection kept and pronoun unchanged.","Pronoun \"I\" should become \"he\"."]},
  {"id":"narration-05","sec":"verbal","topic":"narration","pattern":"direct-indirect-questions","d":"Medium","q":"Choose the correct reported speech for the sentence.\n\nShe said to him, \"Have you finished the work?\"","opts":["She asked him that had he finished the work.","She asked him whether you had finished the work.","She asked him if he had finished the work.","She asked him if he has finished the work."],"ans":2,"time":50,"sol":["Yes/no questions take \"if/whether\" and a past perfect after backshift."],"short":"Yes/no questions take \"if/whether\" and a past perfect after backshift.","trap":"Adding \"that\" or keeping inversion.","why":["\"that\" with inversion.","\"you\" should become \"he\".",null,"Not backshifted."]},
  {"id":"fb-01","sec":"verbal","topic":"fill-blanks","pattern":"vocabulary","d":"Easy","q":"Despite repeated warnings, he remained ______ to the dangers of the shortcut.","opts":["attentive","sensitive","oblivious","vigilant"],"ans":2,"time":35,"sol":["\"Despite warnings\" signals he did not notice the danger, so *oblivious* (unaware) fits."],"why":["Attentive means alert, the opposite of what \"despite warnings\" implies","Sensitive to danger would mean he heeded the warnings",null,"Vigilant means watchful, so it contradicts \"despite warnings\""]},
  {"id":"fb-03","sec":"verbal","topic":"fill-blanks","pattern":"grammar","d":"Easy","q":"Neither the manager nor the engineers ______ aware of the change when the system crashed.","opts":["was","is","has been","were"],"ans":3,"time":35,"sol":["With neither…nor the verb agrees with the nearer subject (engineers), plural, in past tense: *were*."],"why":["Verb agrees with the nearer subject, \"engineers\" (plural)","Wrong tense and number for a past event","Present perfect clashes with \"when the system crashed\"",null]},
  {"id":"fb-05","sec":"verbal","topic":"fill-blanks","pattern":"double-blanks","d":"Medium","q":"The ______ weather forced the airline to ______ all evening flights.","opts":["pleasant … operate","severe … cancel","mild … delay","cheerful … cancel"],"ans":1,"time":55,"sol":["Only bad weather can force an airline to cancel flights, so *severe … cancel*."],"why":["Pleasant weather would not force any change",null,"Mild weather would not force a delay","Weather is not described as cheerful, and the first blank fails"]},
  {"id":"fb-07","sec":"verbal","topic":"fill-blanks","pattern":"collocations","d":"Easy","q":"He ______ a mistake in the final calculation.","opts":["made","did","took","gave"],"ans":0,"time":35,"sol":["The fixed collocation is *make a mistake*."],"why":[null,"\"Do a mistake\" is not idiomatic","\"Take a mistake\" is not idiomatic","\"Give a mistake\" is not idiomatic"]},
  {"id":"fb-08","sec":"verbal","topic":"fill-blanks","pattern":"phrasal-verbs","d":"Medium","q":"The negotiations ______ after the two sides failed to agree on pricing.","opts":["broke out","broke into","broke down","broke in"],"ans":2,"time":55,"sol":["*Broke down* means failed or collapsed, used for talks and machines."],"why":["Break out is used for wars, fires or disease","Break into means enter by force",null,"Break in means enter by force or interrupt"]},
  {"id":"vocab-01","sec":"verbal","topic":"vocabulary","pattern":"synonyms","d":"Easy","q":"Choose the word closest in meaning to **ABUNDANT**.","opts":["scarce","plentiful","rare","limited"],"ans":1,"time":35,"sol":["*Abundant* means existing in large quantity, i.e. plentiful."],"why":["Opposite in meaning",null,"Opposite in meaning","Opposite in meaning"]},
  {"id":"vocab-02","sec":"verbal","topic":"vocabulary","pattern":"antonyms","d":"Easy","q":"Choose the word opposite in meaning to **GENEROUS**.","opts":["stingy","kind","lavish","noble"],"ans":0,"time":35,"sol":["*Generous* means giving freely; its opposite is *stingy*."],"why":[null,"Kind is close in meaning, not opposite","Lavish is similar in meaning","Noble is not the opposite"]},
  {"id":"vocab-04","sec":"verbal","topic":"vocabulary","pattern":"idioms-and-phrases","d":"Medium","q":"Choose the meaning of the idiom **\"to bite the bullet\"**.","opts":["to eat in a hurry","to lose one's temper quickly","to refuse to listen to advice","to face an unpleasant situation with courage"],"ans":3,"time":55,"sol":["*Bite the bullet* means to endure something painful bravely."],"why":["A literal reading","Confused with \"flying off the handle\"","Confused with \"turning a deaf ear\"",null]},
  {"id":"vocab-05","sec":"verbal","topic":"vocabulary","pattern":"confused-words","d":"Medium","q":"Fill in the blank: Honesty is the guiding ______ of our firm.","opts":["principal","principle","principals","principles"],"ans":1,"time":55,"sol":["*Principle* means a rule or belief; *principal* means chief or head."],"why":["\"Principal\" means head of a school or chief (as an adjective, main)",null,"Plural of principal, and the wrong word","Plural; \"the guiding ___ of\" takes the singular here"]},
  {"id":"vocab-06","sec":"verbal","topic":"vocabulary","pattern":"spellings","d":"Medium","q":"Choose the correctly spelt word.","opts":["accomodation","acommodation","accommodation","acomodation"],"ans":2,"time":55,"sol":["*Accommodation* has double c and double m."],"why":["Missing the double m","Missing a c and an m",null,"Missing a c and an m"]},
  {"id":"cloze-01","sec":"verbal","topic":"cloze","pattern":"grammar-based","d":"Easy","q":"Choose the word that best fills blank **(1)** in the passage.","opts":["shares","sharing","share","shared"],"ans":2,"time":35,"sol":["Plural subject *They* with a habitual present: *share*."],"why":["Subject \"They\" is plural","Needs a finite verb here",null,"Past tense clashes with the present-tense passage"],"set":"cloze-1"},
  {"id":"cloze-02","sec":"verbal","topic":"cloze","pattern":"vocabulary-based","d":"Medium","q":"Choose the word that best fills blank **(2)** in the passage.","opts":["pay","give","take","put"],"ans":0,"time":55,"sol":["*Pay off* means bring a good result."],"why":[null,"\"Give off\" has a different meaning","\"Take off\" has a different meaning","\"Put off\" means postpone"],"set":"cloze-1"},
  {"id":"cloze-03","sec":"verbal","topic":"cloze","pattern":"grammar-based","d":"Easy","q":"Choose the word that best fills blank **(3)** in the passage.","opts":["high","higher","highest","more high"],"ans":1,"time":35,"sol":["Comparison with *than* needs the comparative: *higher*."],"why":["Positive degree cannot take \"than\"",null,"Superlative cannot take \"than\"","Short adjectives take -er, not \"more\""],"set":"cloze-1"},
  {"id":"cloze-04","sec":"verbal","topic":"cloze","pattern":"vocabulary-based","d":"Medium","q":"Choose the word that best fills blank **(4)** in the passage.","opts":["pass","raise","hold","take"],"ans":3,"time":55,"sol":["*Take up* means occupy or use (up most of the salary)."],"why":["\"Pass up\" means decline","\"Raise up\" does not fit","\"Hold up\" means delay or rob",null],"set":"cloze-1"},
  {"id":"cloze-07","sec":"verbal","topic":"cloze","pattern":"grammar-based","d":"Medium","q":"Choose the word that best fills blank **(7)** in the passage.","opts":["helps","helping","helped","help"],"ans":3,"time":55,"sol":["Modal + base verb: *can help*."],"why":["After the modal \"can\" the verb stays in the base form","After a modal the base form is needed","After a modal the base form is needed",null],"set":"cloze-1"},
  {"id":"pj-01","sec":"verbal","topic":"para-jumbles","pattern":"opening-sentence","d":"Easy","q":"Rearrange the sentences into a coherent paragraph. Choose the correct order.\n\nP. The kettle began to whistle.\nQ. Ravi put water to boil on the stove.\nR. He poured it over the tea leaves.\nS. Soon the kitchen smelled of fresh tea.","opts":["PQRS","QRPS","QPRS","SQPR"],"ans":2,"time":35,"sol":["Q starts the action; the whistle (P) follows, then \"He poured it\" (R) and the result (S)."],"why":["Breaks the logical or pronoun link","Breaks the logical or pronoun link",null,"Breaks the logical or pronoun link"]},
  {"id":"pj-02","sec":"verbal","topic":"para-jumbles","pattern":"connectors-and-time-order","d":"Easy","q":"Rearrange the sentences into a coherent paragraph. Choose the correct order.\n\nP. This is why many students prefer to study at night.\nQ. The city is noisy during the day.\nR. At night, however, the streets fall quiet.\nS. Traffic, hawkers and construction make concentration hard.","opts":["QSRP","QRSP","SQRP","RQSP"],"ans":0,"time":35,"sol":["Q states the problem, S explains it, R contrasts with \"however\", P concludes with \"This is why\"."],"why":[null,"Breaks the logical or pronoun link","Breaks the logical or pronoun link","Breaks the logical or pronoun link"]},
  {"id":"pj-03","sec":"verbal","topic":"para-jumbles","pattern":"mandatory-pairs","d":"Medium","q":"Rearrange the sentences into a coherent paragraph. Choose the correct order.\n\nP. A young man named Arjun found a wallet on the road.\nQ. Inside it were ₹5,000 and an identity card.\nR. He traced the address on the card and returned the wallet.\nS. The owner, an old teacher, thanked him with tears in his eyes.","opts":["PRQS","QPRS","PQSR","PQRS"],"ans":3,"time":55,"sol":["\"it\" in Q refers to the wallet in P, \"the card\" in R to Q, and the owner thanks him last."],"why":["Breaks the logical or pronoun link","Breaks the logical or pronoun link","Breaks the logical or pronoun link",null]},
  {"id":"pj-04","sec":"verbal","topic":"para-jumbles","pattern":"fixed-first-and-last","d":"Medium","q":"The first and last sentences are fixed. Choose the correct order of the middle sentences.\n\n1. A good leader listens before speaking.\nP. By doing this, he makes the team feel respected.\nQ. He asks colleagues for ideas and notes their concerns.\nR. When decisions are finally taken, people support them willingly.\n6. That is how trust is built.","opts":["PQR","QPR","QRP","RQP"],"ans":1,"time":55,"sol":["Q gives the action, \"By doing this\" (P) refers to it, and R gives the outcome."],"why":["\"By doing this\" needs the action in Q before it",null,"P must follow Q, before the decisions","Decisions cannot come before the consultation"]},
  {"id":"pj-05","sec":"verbal","topic":"para-jumbles","pattern":"odd-sentence-out","d":"Easy","q":"Four of the sentences below form a theme. Pick the sentence that does not belong.\n\nP. A balanced diet includes proteins, vitamins and minerals.\nQ. Regular exercise keeps the heart strong.\nR. Sleeping eight hours helps the body recover.\nS. The new cricket stadium has a seating capacity of 50,000.","opts":["P","Q","R","S"],"ans":3,"time":35,"sol":["P, Q and R are about staying healthy; S is about a stadium."],"why":["Part of the health theme","Part of the health theme","Part of the health theme",null]},
  {"id":"pc-01","sec":"verbal","topic":"para-completion","pattern":"last-sentence","d":"Easy","q":"Choose the sentence that best completes the paragraph.\n\nMany students spend hours on their phones every night. They sleep late and wake up tired. Their concentration in class suffers as a result. ______","opts":["Phones were invented in the twentieth century.","Limiting screen time at night can therefore improve both sleep and studies.","Some students prefer to study in the library.","Teachers should stop giving homework."],"ans":1,"time":35,"sol":["The paragraph lays out a problem, so the ending should give the logical remedy."],"why":["Off-topic fact",null,"Does not follow from the problem described","Unsupported and irrelevant to the cause described"]},
  {"id":"pc-03","sec":"verbal","topic":"para-completion","pattern":"middle-sentence","d":"Medium","q":"Choose the sentence that best fills the gap in the paragraph.\n\nRohan was always late to work. ______ His manager finally warned him that another delay would cost him the job. After that, Rohan began leaving home an hour earlier.","opts":["His colleagues noticed it and complained about the extra work they had to cover.","The office is located near a busy market.","Rohan enjoys cricket on weekends.","His salary was paid on the first of each month."],"ans":0,"time":55,"sol":["The gap must show the consequence of lateness that leads to the manager's warning."],"why":[null,"Does not lead to the manager's warning","Irrelevant","Irrelevant"]},
  {"id":"pc-05","sec":"verbal","topic":"para-completion","pattern":"logical-conclusion","d":"Medium","q":"Choose the conclusion that is best supported by the passage.\n\nAll employees who completed the training were promoted. Kavya was not promoted.","opts":["Kavya completed the training but was unlucky.","Kavya did not complete the training.","Kavya will be promoted next year.","The training was useless."],"ans":1,"time":55,"sol":["If completion guarantees promotion, no promotion means she did not complete it."],"why":["Contradicts the rule: completion means promotion",null,"Not supported by the statements","Not supported by the statements"]},
  {"id":"pc-02","sec":"verbal","topic":"para-completion","pattern":"last-sentence","d":"Easy","q":"Choose the sentence that best completes the paragraph.\n\nMany rivers in India are polluted by untreated sewage and factory waste. This harms fish and the people who depend on them. ______","opts":["Fish are sold in markets across India.","Rivers have inspired poets for centuries.","Factories should be built close to rivers.","Treating waste before it is released is therefore essential."],"ans":3,"time":35,"sol":["The paragraph describes pollution and harm, so the ending states the needed action."],"why":["Off-topic","Off-topic","Would worsen the problem",null]},
  {"id":"pc-04","sec":"verbal","topic":"para-completion","pattern":"middle-sentence","d":"Medium","q":"Choose the sentence that best fills the gap in the paragraph.\n\nOnline shopping has changed how Indians buy things. ______ However, many buyers still worry about receiving damaged goods or being cheated. Easy return policies are therefore a major factor in building trust.","opts":["Shopping malls were first built in America.","Bargaining is a common practice in street markets.","It offers lower prices, wider choice and delivery to the doorstep.","Delivery workers often have long shifts."],"ans":2,"time":55,"sol":["\"However\" needs a positive point before it, then the worry about trust follows."],"why":["Off-topic and does not set up \"However\"","Does not set up the contrast with \"However\"",null,"Does not set up the contrast with \"However\""]},
  {"id":"wr-01","sec":"verbal","topic":"writing","pattern":"email-writing","d":"Easy","q":"Which subject line is best for an email to HR requesting leave from 12 to 14 November?","opts":["Hello","URGENT!!!","Leave request: 12–14 November","Please read"],"ans":2,"time":35,"sol":["A good subject line states the purpose and the key details briefly."],"why":["Vague; does not state the purpose","Shouting; says nothing about the content",null,"Vague; does not state the purpose"]},
  {"id":"wr-03","sec":"verbal","topic":"writing","pattern":"email-writing","d":"Medium","q":"You must complain to a vendor that order #4521, due on 5 March, has not arrived. Which opening is most appropriate?","opts":["Hey, where is my stuff?","You people are useless; deliver my order now.","Sorry to bother you, maybe you could look at something someday.","I am writing to report that order #4521, due on 5 March, has not yet been delivered."],"ans":3,"time":55,"sol":["A complaint should be polite, state the purpose and give order details."],"why":["Too casual","Rude and aggressive","Vague, no details given",null]},
  {"id":"wr-06","sec":"verbal","topic":"writing","pattern":"essay-planning","d":"Easy","q":"Where should the thesis statement (your main position) normally appear in an essay?","opts":["In the introduction","Only in the last line of the conclusion","In the middle of the body","It is not needed in an essay"],"ans":0,"time":35,"sol":["The introduction should tell the reader what the essay will argue."],"why":[null,"Too late; the reader needs it early","Not where readers expect it","Without it the essay lacks focus"]},
  {"id":"wr-09","sec":"verbal","topic":"writing","pattern":"communication-test-tips","d":"Hard","q":"In a Versant-style passage reconstruction task you read a short passage and then retell it. What is the best approach?","opts":["Try to recall every word exactly","Retell only the last sentence","Note the main idea and key details, then retell them in your own words in order","Add your own opinions to the story"],"ans":2,"time":80,"sol":["The task rewards the meaning and sequence of ideas, not word-for-word recall."],"why":["Not possible and not required","Misses most of the passage",null,"Retelling must stay faithful to the passage"]},
  {"id":"wr-07","sec":"verbal","topic":"writing","pattern":"essay-planning","d":"Medium","q":"In a 20-minute essay on \"Social media: boon or bane?\", what is the best first step?","opts":["Start writing immediately to save time","Memorise a long quotation and use it","Write only the conclusion first","Spend 2–3 minutes listing points for both sides and arranging them in order"],"ans":3,"time":55,"sol":["A short outline keeps the essay balanced and well ordered."],"why":["Leads to an unorganised essay","Does not plan the content","Not a planning step",null]}
]);
QB.set("rc-1", {"title":"Passage 1: Automation and the office","kind":"passage","text":"When spreadsheets first arrived in offices in the 1980s, many accountants feared for their jobs. Instead, the opposite happened: because calculations became cheaper, firms asked for more of them, and the number of financial analysts grew. This history is often quoted by those who believe that the current wave of workplace automation will also create more work than it destroys. They may be right, but the comparison hides an important difference.\n\nSpreadsheets sped up a task that a human still had to frame, check and interpret. Today's software, by contrast, can draft reports, sort job applications and answer customer queries with very little human involvement. The skills it replaces are not only routine ones; some belong to the middle rung of the career ladder, where junior employees traditionally learned their trade by doing repetitive tasks under supervision. If those tasks vanish, companies may find themselves with plenty of senior experts but no obvious way to train the next generation of them.\n\nNone of this means that organisations should resist the technology. Firms that have adopted it carefully report faster turnaround and fewer clerical errors. The sensible response is to redesign entry-level roles so that they include review and judgement from the start, rather than leaving those for later. Employees, for their part, would do well to treat tools as collaborators whose output must always be verified. A confident but wrong answer from a machine is no more acceptable than one from a colleague.\n\nThe lesson of the spreadsheet, then, is not that technology is harmless, but that its effects depend on how people choose to organise work around it."});
QB.set("cloze-1", {"title":"Cloze passage: graduates in the city","kind":"passage","text":"Every year, thousands of young graduates move to big cities in search of work. They **(1)** ______ small rented rooms with several roommates, hoping that the sacrifice will **(2)** ______ off in the long run. Many of them soon discover that the cost of living is **(3)** ______ than they had expected. Rent, food and transport together **(4)** ______ up most of a fresh graduate's salary, leaving little to save. Yet most **(5)** ______ in the city, because the opportunities for learning are **(6)** ______ greater than in their hometowns.\n\nA good manager can **(7)** ______ a new employee grow quickly, provided that the employee is willing to listen. Those who **(8)** ______ criticism as a chance to improve usually rise faster than those who take it **(9)** ______. In the end, patience and steady effort **(10)** ______ more than a high starting salary."});
